{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FRHnYvGnqjTFR2S9qQbB9b","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Prototype: JQ Syntax Generator\n2\t\n3\t## Problem\n4\tWriting `jq` paths by hand against deeply nested JSON is error-prone and slow. Users need a visual way to click into a JSON structure and get the correct filter expression immediately.\n5\t\n6\t## Approach\n7\t- Serve a single `index.html` from a Node.js `http` server (`server.js`)\n8\t- `<textarea>` for JSON input with a \"Parse\" button\n9\t- On parse, recursively build a `<ul>`/`<li>` tree from the JSON object using vanilla JS\n10\t- Each `<li>` element stores its full jq path in a `data-path` attribute, built at render time\n11\t- On click, read `data-path` and write it to a read-only `<input>` with a copy button\n12\t- Path building rules: object key → `.key`, array index → `.[N]`, nested → concatenate\n13\t\n14\t## Data / State\n15\t- `currentJson` — the last successfully parsed JSON object (in-memory only)\n16\t- `selectedPath` — the currently displayed jq path string\n17\t- No server-side state; all state lives in the browser\n18\t\n19\t## Open Questions\n20\t- How to handle keys with spaces or special characters? Needs bracket notation: `.[\"key with space\"]`\n21\tResponse: use bracketed notation\n22\t- Should the tool validate the generated path against the JSON (test mode)? Out of scope for prototype.\n23\tResponse: yes have the tool test\n24\t- Render strategy for very large JSON (10k+ keys) — virtual scrolling may be needed but skip for v1.\n25\tResponse: json file will be small. \n26\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94a4313b-6221-4e8f-a88e-c12acac2a892","uuid":"1e546ed0-ea47-4271-b6bf-f341d6e213ba","timestamp":"2026-04-23T04:06:16.356Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/sysoper/workspace/WhileISleep/batch-output/jq-syntax-generator/prototype.md","content":"# Prototype: JQ Syntax Generator\n\n## Problem\nWriting `jq` paths by hand against deeply nested JSON is error-prone and slow. Users need a visual way to click into a JSON structure and get the correct filter expression immediately.\n\n## Approach\n- Serve a single `index.html` from a Node.js `http` server (`server.js`)\n- `<textarea>` for JSON input with a \"Parse\" button\n- On parse, recursively build a `<ul>`/`<li>` tree from the JSON object using vanilla JS\n- Each `<li>` element stores its full jq path in a `data-path` attribute, built at render time\n- On click, read `data-path` and write it to a read-only `<input>` with a copy button\n- Path building rules: object key → `.key`, array index → `.[N]`, nested → concatenate\n\n## Data / State\n- `currentJson` — the last successfully parsed JSON object (in-memory only)\n- `selectedPath` — the currently displayed jq path string\n- No server-side state; all state lives in the browser\n\n## Open Questions\n- How to handle keys with spaces or special characters? Needs bracket notation: `.[\"key with space\"]`\nResponse: use bracketed notation\n- Should the tool validate the generated path against the JSON (test mode)? Out of scope for prototype.\nResponse: yes have the tool test\n- Render strategy for very large JSON (10k+ keys) — virtual scrolling may be needed but skip for v1.\nResponse: json file will be small. \n","numLines":26,"startLine":1,"totalLines":26}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-4-6","id":"msg_01JGNLHqUeNFaWu7S1ew2oW8","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have all the context I need. Let me build the prototype:\n\n1. `server.js` - minimal Node.js HTTP server serving the single HTML file\n2. `index.html` - single-page app with:\n   - Textarea for JSON input\n   - Parse button\n   - Tree view with clickable nodes\n   - Path display with copy button\n3. `Dockerfile` - using node:22-alpine\n4. `.dockerignore`\n\nKey requirements from CLAUDE.md:\n- Node.js `http` and `fs` built-ins only — no Express, no bundler\n- Explicit over clever\n\n\n- Immutable-by-default with `const` and pure functions\n- No silent error swallowing — JSON parsing errors shown inline\n- Array indices using `.[0]` notation\n- Both auto-populate text field AND copy button\n- Keys with spaces/special chars using bracket notation like `.[\"key with space\"]`\n- Test mode to validate the generated path against the JSON\n\nFor the server, I'll use just `http` and `fs` modules to serve `index.html` on GET / and return 404 for other paths, listening on port 3000. The HTML file will be a single-page app handling all the tree parsing and path generation logic.\n\nBuilding the HTML with embedded CSS and JavaScript to render the JSON as a recursive tree structure, where each node has a `data-path` attribute storing its full jq path. Clicking a node populates the input field with that path and tests it against the current JSON to verify it's valid. The path building logic handles simple identifiers as `.key`, special characters with bracket notation, and array indices as `.[0]`, then concatenates them for nested access. identifier → append `.key`; otherwise → append `[\"key\"]`\n- Array index: append `.[N]`\n\n**Path test implementation:**\n- Parse the path and traverse the JSON to verify the path resolves to a value\n- Show the result (value or \"invalid path\")\n\nLet me implement a simple path parser/evaluator:\n\nThe path evaluator needs to:\n1. Start with the root JSON\n2. Parse the jq path and traverse it\n\nFor simplicity, since we're building the paths ourselves, I can evaluate them by traversing the same data structure with the stored path.\n\nActually, a cleaner approach: each `<li>` stores its path as `data-path`, and when clicked I can re-traverse the JSON using the path to verify it. But actually, since I built the path during rendering, I know it's valid. The \"test\" feature might mean showing what value the path resolves to.\n\nRe-reading the requirement: \"Should the tool validate the generated path against the JSON (test mode)? Response: yes have the tool test\" — I think this means displaying the resolved value when you click a path, essentially testing that the path returns the correct value. I'll show the resolved value in the UI.\n\nSetting up the server...\n\nBuilding the HTML interface... Writing the JavaScript logic... Writing the path parser...\n\nActually, I'm realizing this approach is getting unwieldy. Since I already know the structure when building the paths, I could store the resolved keys alongside the jq expression itself, but that would complicate the data model. A simpler approach might be to just stringify the resolved values for display and focus on getting the jq evaluation working correctly.\n\nWait, I'm overcomplicating this. Each node in the tree already has access to its actual data value, not just the path. I could store the value directly in the node, though that might bloat the data attributes if the JSON is large. The real solution is cleaner: when a user clicks a node, I'll evaluate the path string against the current JSON using a dedicated path evaluator function that understands the path formats I'm generating.\n\nWriting path evaluator... Writing the tokenizer... Writing path building logic... I need to handle the path concatenation carefully—if the parent path is already \".\", I shouldn't just concatenate the segment directly or I'll end up with \"..foo\". The solution is to check if parentPath is \".\" and handle it specially, otherwise just append the segment to the parent path.\n\nImplementing the path builder... Building the tree view structure... Writing the tree component... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Still writing styles... Writing the path builder logic... Writing the path parser... Writing the path traversal logic... Still writing the renderer... Building the tree structure... Writing component code... Setting up the parse button handler to validate the JSON input, check that it's an object or array at the root level, and then store it for path resolution while clearing any previous selections or output.\n\nNow I'm adding event listeners for the copy button that writes the selected path to the clipboard, with a fallback to the older copy method for browsers that don't support the clipboard API, and showing a temporary confirmation message.\n\nI'm realizing there's an issue with how I'm rendering the root node. The `renderNode` function handles null keys for the root, but I need to make sure the root displays correctly without trying to override it afterward. Let me think through the logic: when the key is null, the node won't have a key label, which is actually fine since the root is implicitly the top level. I should just let the existing logic handle it naturally rather than adding special cases. I need to think through the root node rendering—when the path is \".\", clicking it should select the whole document, which is the correct jq path. I'm also reconsidering how to handle primitives at the root level, though I've already filtered those out during parsing. Now I'm looking at the Dockerfile setup and realizing that with `--network none`, I can't access the server from outside, so the CMD needs to either start the server and run an internal verification, or just execute a unit test of the JavaScript logic instead.\n\nSince this is a web app, I need a way to demonstrate it works within the container itself. I could start the server in the background, use wget to fetch the HTML and verify the response, then exit cleanly. Alternatively, I could write a separate test script that imports and validates the server logic without needing to make HTTP requests.\n\nWriting test script... Writing test runner...\n\nNow I'm thinking about whether I can actually run the `evaluatePath` function in Node.js for testing, since it's browser-compatible code. The simplest approach would be to create a test file that starts the server, makes an HTTP request to verify the response. I'm also reconsidering the `--network none` flag on Docker—it removes all network interfaces except loopback, so localhost should still be accessible within the container for testing purposes.\n\nMy plan is to use a shell script as the CMD that starts the server in the background, waits a moment for it to boot, makes a request to localhost to verify it's working, then cleans up and exits with the appropriate status code.\n\nActually, a simpler approach would be to write a Node script that spawns the server process, waits for it to signal it's ready, makes an HTTP request to verify the response contains the expected content, then exits.\n\nWriting test script... I'm realizing I'm overcomplicating the testing approach—let me simplify by using a shell script in the Dockerfile that starts the server, waits briefly, makes a quick request to verify it's working, and then cleans up. If any step fails, the exit code will reflect that.\n\nActually, a proper test.js file makes more sense. I'll write a test that includes the core path-building logic inline and validates it works correctly.\n\nWriting test logic... Writing unit tests...\n\nOnce the unit tests pass, I'm spinning up the server and making an HTTP request to verify it's running correctly—checking that it returns a 200 status code and the expected page content before shutting everything down. I need to handle a few edge cases with the JSON keys—escaping backslashes in bracket notation and making sure numeric keys work properly. The root path should resolve to \".\" for the entire document, which is valid jq syntax. Since the implementation is fully client-side, I'm meeting the privacy requirement, and I'll validate the generated paths by evaluating them against the JSON to show the resolved values. Now I'm ready to write out all the files cleanly.\n\nBuilding the path tokenizer...\n\nFor the path tokenizer, I need to handle:\n- `.foo` → key: \"foo\"\n- `.[0]` → index: 0\n- `.[\"foo bar\"]` → key: \"foo bar\"\n- Combined: `.foo.[0].[\"bar baz\"].qux`\n\nThe tokenizer starts at position 1 (after the initial \".\"). Then:\n- If char is \".\", skip it\n- If char is \"[\", look for \"]\"\n  - If inner starts with `\"`, it's a bracket key\n  - Otherwise, it's an array index\n- Otherwise, read until \".\" or \"[\" → it's a key\n\nI always put a `.` before `[` for array indices: `.[0]`, `.foo.[0]`, `.foo.[0].bar`. Let me trace through \".foo.[0].bar\" to verify the logic works: starting at position 1, I read \"foo\" until hitting the dot, then skip the dot and bracket, parse the index 0, skip the next dot, and read \"bar\" to the end. For '.[\"foo bar\"]', I'd skip the initial dot, find the bracket, and extract the quoted string as a key.\n\nNow I'm setting up the project structure with a minimal HTTP server, a full single-page app, unit tests with server integration, and Docker configuration.\n\nI should clarify the root node behavior—clicking it should select the path \".\" and display the entire JSON document, consistent with how jq works. For the UI interaction model, I'll separate the clickable label (which selects the path) from the toggle arrow (which expands or collapses branches), so clicking a node selects its path without accidentally toggling visibility.\n\nWriting the files now...\n\nFor the HTML structure, I'm planning a two-section layout: the top has a textarea for JSON input with a parse button and error display, while the bottom splits into a left panel showing the tree and a right panel displaying the selected path and test results. I'll use flexbox to make it responsive across different screen sizes, and add a sample JSON button to help users get started.\n\nI need to be careful about error handling—the guidelines say no silent errors, so I'll make sure every error is visible to the user. For the test file, I could duplicate the utility functions or extract them into a shared module, but since the project uses a single HTML file, I'll keep the pure functions small and duplicate them in the test file rather than complicate the setup.\n\nWriting the files now... I see the Node.js server setup is already described in the current rewritten thinking. Now I'm moving on to build out the HTML file for the single-page application and then create a test file to validate the core logic.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":851,"cache_read_input_tokens":17991,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":851},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"94a4313b-6221-4e8f-a88e-c12acac2a892","uuid":"ed556131-0583-4e9d-9cad-76099c3c084f"}
